# Conformance suite for internal/bid

The files `*.txt` in this directory fix the expected result of every
operation of package `internal/bid` at every boundary that the package
documentation (`doc.go`, the contract of the package) names. They were
written from that documentation and from the text of IEEE 754-2008, by
hand or with small generators that share no code with the kernel or with
the reference oracle in `ref_test.go`. Nothing here was copied or
transcribed from decTest, from Intel's test suite or from any other
library's tests, and no expected result was computed with another decimal
library. The suite is therefore an independent check: when it disagrees
with the kernel or the oracle, the disagreement is settled against the
contract and the standard, not against this suite.

`conformance_test.go` reads the suite and runs it against the kernel in
`TestConformance`. A vector whose operation still panics with
`internal/bid: not implemented` is skipped and counted per operation;
every other mismatch fails. `TestConformanceReference` in
`agreement_test.go` runs the same suite against the reference oracle, with
no skip for an unimplemented operation. A vector whose disagreement waits
on a decision about the contract may be listed there by position, with its
reason; a listed vector that passes fails the test, so a stale entry
cannot linger. `TestConformanceREADME` checks the table at the
end of this file against the suite, so the table cannot drift.

## Format

A vector is one line:

    Op mode class operand... -> result...

Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored. Tokens are separated
by spaces; a Go quoted string (`" 1"`, `"1\n"`) is one token.

`Op` is the exported function, width suffix included (`Add64`, `Narrow`,
`Int64From128`). `mode` is the rounding mode:

| token | Mode |
|---|---|
| `even` | ToNearestEven |
| `away` | ToNearestAway |
| `zero` | TowardZero |
| `up` | TowardPositive |
| `down` | TowardNegative |
| a number | `Mode(n)`; the contract says any value outside the five behaves as ToNearestEven |
| `-` | the operation takes no mode |

`class` is the boundary class the vector exercises (next section). The
operands and results are typed by the operation's signature:

- A decimal value is written in the character form of the contract
  (`1.50`, `-0E+5`, `1.234567890123456E+16`, `Infinity`, `-Infinity`,
  `NaN`), extended by `-NaN`, `sNaN` and `-sNaN` for the NaN encodings
  the character form does not distinguish. The spelling fixes the
  quantum: `1.50` is coefficient 150 with exponent -2. A value may also
  be written as its bit pattern, `0x` followed by 16 hex digits for
  decimal64 or 32 for decimal128; that is how non-canonical encodings,
  NaN payloads and the results of the sign operations (which copy bits)
  are stated. A result written in either form is compared bit for bit, so
  `NaN` as a result means the canonical quiet NaN and `0` means +0 with
  exponent 0.
- A `Status` is `Exact`, or the flag names joined with `|`:
  `Inexact|Overflow`.
- Integers (`int64`, `uint64`, `int` exponents, `Uint128` coefficients)
  are decimal; booleans are `true` and `false`; a `Kind` is `finite`,
  `inf` or `nan`.
- A `Key` result is four tokens: `coeff exp kind neg`.
- A binary float is a Go literal read with Go's semantics (the nearest
  float64 or float32), including the hexadecimal form (`0x1p-24`), with
  `+Inf`, `-Inf`, `NaN` and `-0`. The exact value of every binary operand
  that is not exactly representable in decimal is given in a comment. A
  `NaN` result is the quiet NaN with the sign bit clear and a zero
  payload, the only NaN the contract lets a conversion produce.
- A string (Parse operand, Append result) is a Go quoted string.

The operands follow the Go signature in order. `Round64 even tie false
12345678901234565 0 false -> 1.234567890123456E+16 Inexact` is
`Round64(false, 12345678901234565, 0, false, ToNearestEven)`.

## Boundary classes

Every vector names one class. The classes, and what a vector in each one
exercises:

| class | exercises |
|---|---|
| `exact` | an exact result: the preferred exponent, or the cohort member nearest it when the preferred exponent is not available |
| `inexact` | a result that discards a non-tie fraction, under the modes that differ |
| `tie` | a result exactly halfway between two representable values, under all five modes; for Rem, a quotient halfway between two integers |
| `carry` | a rounding that carries into a new digit (`9999999999999999` + 1), or into the next binade for a binary result |
| `overflow` | a result too large for the format: an infinity or the largest finite value by mode and sign, including the cases decided only after rounding; for Pack, an operand it refuses |
| `underflow` | the subnormal edge: exact subnormals, inexact subnormals, tiny results rounding to zero and tiny results rounding up to the smallest normal |
| `clamp` | an exact result whose exponent would leave `[MinExp, MaxExp]` and is re-encoded at the bound, and zeros at the bounds |
| `zero` | the sign and exponent of a zero result, zero operands, and a zero divisor |
| `inf` | infinite operands and results, including the invalid operations on infinities |
| `nan` | NaN operands of every kind (quiet, signaling, negative, with payload, non-canonical) and NaN results, including the invalid operations that produce them |
| `noncanon` | non-canonical encodings as operands: a coefficient field above `MaxCoeff`, a second-form decimal128, an infinity with coefficient bits |
| `grammar` | strings at the edge of the Parse grammar: everything it must reject with `Syntax`, and the accepted spellings next to them |

A `-` in the table below declares that a class does not apply to an
operation, and the test requires such a cell to have no vectors:

- `grammar` applies only to Parse, and `noncanon` not to Parse, Round or
  the conversions from binary or integer operands, whose inputs are not
  encodings.
- The exact operations (Rem, Mod, Reduce, SameQuantum, Min, Max, the
  comparisons, Key, Widen, Append, Unpack, Pack, the predicates, the sign
  operations and the decimal128 integer conversions) have no `inexact`,
  `tie` or `carry` class, except that Rem has `tie` for a halfway
  quotient. They cannot overflow; Reduce has `clamp` because its exponent
  stops at MaxExp.
- RoundToIntegral and Quantize have a fixed result exponent, so they
  neither overflow nor clamp (a Quantize whose coefficient would not fit
  is a NaN, in `nan`).
- Division has no `carry` class: a quotient of two coefficients of at most
  Precision digits is never within one unit in the last place below a
  power of ten unless it is exact (write the quotient as A/B with A =
  10^k B - r; the required closeness forces B > 10^(Precision-k) while A
  < 10^Precision forces B < 10^(Precision-k)).
- `Float64To128`, `Float32To64` and `Float32To128` have no `carry` class:
  the floats just below every power of ten in range were enumerated and
  none has 34 (float64) or 16 (float32) leading nines. The conversions
  from binary to decimal cannot overflow, underflow or clamp, since every
  float64 is a normal decimal64 value; those from integers likewise.
- Round takes a value in parts, so it has no `inf`, `nan` or `noncanon`
  operand class; its infinite results are in `overflow`.
- The conversions to integers and to binary have no `clamp` class, since
  their results have no decimal exponent.

